  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13986308/assets/0c4cf0ae740f876576ff140f488432dbfae9d26eb5e68e9bafbcc9b5a5bb286b.png)\n\nWhat is the total surface area of the rectangular prism above if it is $4\\,\\text{cm}$ long, $3\\,\\text{cm}$ wide, and $3\\,\\text{cm}$ tall?",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's consider the net of our plank.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13986308/assets/98eb9b74a15ad1cad75974496a370fb9b9b28f7ff57bc319500fcd31492216d3.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have squares and rectangles.\n\nThe rectangles are $4\\,\\text{cm}$ long and $3\\,\\text{cm}$ high. So, the area of one rectangle is:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area of the rectangle}=4\\times 3=12\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe squares has the side equal to $3\\,\\text{cm}.$ Hence, the area of one square is:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area of the square}=3\\times 3=9\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the surface area of the rectangular prism consists of $4$ rectangles and $2$ squares. \n\nTherefore, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Surface area} & =4\\times (\\text{Area of the rectangle})+2\\times (\\text{Area of the square}) \\\\  & =4\\times 12+2\\times 9 \\\\  & =48+18 \\\\  & =66\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
